export const stages = [
 {id:'arithmetic',n:'01',title:'Arithmetic',tag:'Make numbers familiar',time:'Begin here if fractions or negative numbers feel shaky.',idea:'Numbers describe amounts. Operations describe what happens to those amounts.',why:'Fractions become ratios, rates, and algebraic expressions. Comfortable arithmetic frees your attention for the new idea in an engineering problem.',topics:['Place value and the four operations','Fractions: meaning, equivalence, all four operations','Decimals, percentages, ratios and proportions','Negative numbers, order of operations and estimation'],check:['Add, subtract, multiply and divide fractions without a calculator.','Explain what a percentage means and convert between fractions, decimals and percentages.','Estimate an answer before calculating and notice an implausible result.'],example:'If a small pump moves 3/4 of a liter per minute, in 2 minutes it moves 3/2 liters. A fraction is a usable quantity, not just a school exercise.',book:'open-pre',practice:'khan-arithmetic',chapters:'Start with whole numbers only if needed. In OpenStax Prealgebra, concentrate first on integers, fractions, decimals, and percent. Use chapter practice tests to find gaps.',gate:'Advance when mixed arithmetic stays reliable without looking up each step. Speed can develop gradually.'},
 {id:'prealgebra',n:'02',title:'Prealgebra',tag:'Give numbers a language',time:'After basic arithmetic is dependable.',idea:'A variable is a name for a quantity. An equation says two expressions have the same value.',why:'You begin turning a description into mathematics: a distance, an unknown time, a conversion, or a relationship between measurements.',topics:['Variables, expressions and the meaning of equality','Distributive property and combining like terms','Simple equations and translating words into equations','Powers, roots, scientific notation and unit conversions'],check:['Explain the difference between an expression and an equation.','Solve a simple equation and check by substitution.','Convert units while showing why the unwanted units cancel.'],example:'Distance = speed × time becomes d = vt. If d = 120 and v = 30 in compatible units, solving 120 = 30t gives t = 4.',book:'open-pre',practice:'khan-pre',chapters:'Continue the same Prealgebra book: language of algebra, properties, equations, models, exponents, and graphs. Arithmetic and prealgebra overlap; this is one continuous foundation.',gate:'Advance when you can set up and solve a simple unfamiliar word problem, including units.'},
 {id:'algebra',n:'03',title:'Algebra I',tag:'Make relationships work',time:'Once variables and simple equations make sense.',idea:'Algebra lets you transform a relationship while keeping its meaning true.',why:'Engineering formulas rarely arrive with the quantity you need already isolated. Rearranging equations and reading graphs become everyday tools.',topics:['Linear equations, inequalities and formula rearrangement','Slope, coordinate graphs and straight-line models','Systems of equations','Polynomial operations, factoring and introductory quadratics'],check:['Solve equations with parentheses and fractions, then verify the answer.','Rearrange a formula for a different variable.','Connect a line’s equation, table, graph and verbal description.'],example:'In F = ma, dividing both sides by a gives m = F/a when a ≠ 0. The operation is justified by preserving equality, not by “moving a letter.”',book:'open-elementary',practice:'khan-algebra',chapters:'Use Elementary Algebra for the full sequence. Some later topics return in Intermediate Algebra. Diagnose that overlap instead of restarting every topic from scratch.',gate:'Advance when you can choose the method on a mixed set, without the section heading telling you what to do.'},
 {id:'geometry',n:'↗',title:'Geometry alongside algebra',tag:'Make the quantities visible',time:'Work alongside Algebra I and II, as the algebra permits.',idea:'A diagram is another way to describe a relationship.',why:'Geometry connects equations to space: a bracket’s dimensions, a tank’s volume, the distance between points, or the scale of a model.',topics:['Angles, triangles, similarity and scale','Perimeter, area, surface area and volume','Pythagorean theorem and coordinate distance','Circles and introductory right-triangle ratios'],check:['Draw and label a diagram from a written problem.','Use similar triangles or the Pythagorean theorem appropriately.','Distinguish length, area and volume units.'],example:'Doubling every length of a cube multiplies its surface area by 4 and its volume by 8. “Twice as big” needs a more precise meaning.',book:'open-pre',practice:'khan-geometry',chapters:'Begin with OpenStax Prealgebra’s geometry and measurement models. Use selected Khan Geometry units for triangles, similarity, circles, area and volume. Formal proof practice can deepen this later.',gate:'This is a companion strand, not a requirement to finish an entire geometry textbook before touching Algebra II.'},
 {id:'algebra2',n:'04',title:'Algebra II & functions',tag:'Get ready for precalculus',time:'Build on reliable Algebra I, with geometry continuing alongside.',idea:'A function connects inputs to outputs. Its equation, graph and domain describe the same relationship.',why:'Functions become the language of motion, power, temperature, signals and control. Algebraic fluency makes the later calculus much easier to follow.',topics:['Function notation, domain, graphs and transformations','Factoring and solving quadratic equations','Rational expressions, radicals and exponent rules','Systems, complex-number basics, and introductory exponential/logarithmic relationships'],check:['Simplify algebraic fractions and state excluded values.','Solve a quadratic and check which solutions fit the original problem.','Evaluate a function and interpret its graph and domain.','Work through a fresh mixed algebra set without relying on hints.'],example:'For f(t) = 2t + 3, f(4) = 11. In a model, the symbols and units matter: t might be seconds, while f(t) might be a sensor reading.',book:'open-intermediate',practice:'khan-algebra2',chapters:'Use Intermediate Algebra to repair gaps and develop the later topics. Do not repeat all of Elementary Algebra if chapter checks show you already know it.',gate:'This is the main target of this guide: readiness to BEGIN precalculus. The exact entry standard depends on the college course.'},
 {id:'precalculus',n:'05',title:'Precalculus',tag:'The next course, when ready',time:'Begin after the algebra foundation; this is beyond “precalc readiness.”',idea:'You study families of functions deeply and connect algebra with trigonometry.',why:'Radians, trigonometric functions, exponentials and transformations prepare you to describe changing systems in calculus and physics.',topics:['Polynomial, rational, exponential and logarithmic functions','Trigonometry: radians, unit circle, graphs and identities','Inverse and composite functions','Additional topics chosen by your course, such as conics or sequences'],check:['Use the actual course syllabus and placement requirements to choose your starting class.','For calculus readiness later: work fluently with functions, algebra and trigonometry.','Use the assigned college text as the main resource once classes begin.'],example:'A sine wave can describe an idealized oscillation. Trigonometry gives the relationship; calculus will later describe how quickly it changes.',book:'open-precalc',practice:'khan-precalc',chapters:'Choose OpenStax Precalculus OR Stewart/Redlin/Watson’s Precalculus. A large algebra-and-trigonometry book is an alternative route, not another book you must finish too.',gate:'Self-study builds knowledge. It does not itself award credit or satisfy Southwestern’s enrollment requirements.'}
];

